{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01JWrazNUWar8HYjgz85Sjau","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public ABI (from `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n4\t//!   * `ldexp_q2`\n5\t//!\n6\t//! The translation reproduces the original semantics exactly, including the\n7\t//! implementation-defined / undefined behaviour the C code relies on (see the\n8\t//! notes on `ldexp_q2`).\n9\t\n10\t#![allow(clippy::needless_return)]\n11\t\n12\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n13\t\n14\t/// `static const float g_expfrac[4]` from `src/lib.c`.\n15\t///\n16\t/// These are `2^-30 * 2^(-k/4)` for `k = 0..3`.\n17\tconst G_EXPFRAC: [f32; 4] = [\n18\t    9.31322575e-10f32,\n19\t    7.83145814e-10f32,\n20\t    6.58544508e-10f32,\n21\t    5.53767716e-10f32,\n22\t];\n23\t\n24\t/// ```c\n25\t/// float ldexp_q2(float y, int exp_q2) {\n26\t///     static const float g_expfrac[4] = {...};\n27\t///     int e;\n28\t///     do {\n29\t///         e = ((30 * 4) > (exp_q2) ? (exp_q2) : (30 * 4));\n30\t///         y *= g_expfrac[e & 3] * (1 << 30 >> (e >> 2));\n31\t///     } while ((exp_q2 -= e) > 0);\n32\t///     return y;\n33\t/// }\n34\t/// ```\n35\t///\n36\t/// Behavioural notes (faithfully preserved, *not* \"fixed\"):\n37\t///\n38\t/// * `e` is `min(120, exp_q2)`, so it may be negative. `e & 3` then indexes with\n39\t///   the two's-complement low bits (e.g. `-1 & 3 == 3`), which keeps the index\n40\t///   inside the array, and `e >> 2` is an arithmetic shift.\n41\t/// * For a negative `e`, `1 << 30 >> (e >> 2)` shifts by a negative amount,\n42\t///   which is undefined behaviour in C. The C build (gcc, x86-64) emits\n43\t///   `sar %cl`, whose count is taken modulo 32, so the observable behaviour is a\n44\t///   shift by `(e >> 2) & 31`. That is what is reproduced here; masking also\n45\t///   keeps the Rust shift itself well defined.\n46\t/// * The arithmetic is done in `f32` (x86-64 SSE, `FLT_EVAL_METHOD