{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Y7vLicDRFuxk1WkMMPu5hP","type":"tool_result","content":"=== functional lines of src/lib.rs (comments/attrs stripped) ===\n#![allow(clippy::needless_return)]\nuse std::ffi::c_int;\n#[allow(clippy::excessive_precision)]\nconst G_EXPFRAC: [f32; 4] = [\n    9.31322575e-10f32,\n    7.83145814e-10f32,\n    6.58544508e-10f32,\n    5.53767716e-10f32,\n];\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub extern \"C\" fn ldexp_q2(y: f32, exp_q2: c_int) -> f32 {\n    let mut y = y;\n    let mut exp_q2 = exp_q2;\n    loop {\n        let e: c_int = if (30 * 4) > exp_q2 { exp_q2 } else { 30 * 4 };\n        let frac = G_EXPFRAC[(e & 3) as usize];\n        let scale = (1i32 << 30) >> ((e >> 2) & 31);\n        y *= frac * (scale as f32);\n        exp_q2 = exp_q2.wrapping_sub(e);\n        if exp_q2 <= 0 {\n            return y;\n        }\n    }\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fceb0364-53bc-448b-bf88-91d6e77258b6","uuid":"53675c66-7f60-4751-aeeb-c4d0e181b671","timestamp":"2026-08-27T00:06:51.649Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== functional lines of src/lib.rs (comments/attrs stripped) ===\n#![allow(clippy::needless_return)]\nuse std::ffi::c_int;\n#[allow(clippy::excessive_precision)]\nconst G_EXPFRAC: [f32; 4] = [\n    9.31322575e-10f32,\n    7.83145814e-10f32,\n    6.58544508e-10f32,\n    5.53767716e-10f32,\n];\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub extern \"C\" fn ldexp_q2(y: f32, exp_q2: c_int) -> f32 {\n    let mut y = y;\n    let mut exp_q2 = exp_q2;\n    loop {\n        let e: c_int = if (30 * 4) > exp_q2 { exp_q2 } else { 30 * 4 };\n        let frac = G_EXPFRAC[(e & 3) as usize];\n        let scale = (1i32 << 30) >> ((e >> 2) & 31);\n        y *= frac * (scale as f32);\n        exp_q2 = exp_q2.wrapping_sub(e);\n        if exp_q2 <= 0 {\n            return y;\n        }\n    }\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_6b5q4h6oqhn5aeara442tajmett6purkwhm4tbnlw6d7prgiovda","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. The translation's logic is byte-identical to what I was given — I changed no Rust logic, only the test harness, docs, and one `#[allow]`.\n\n## Result\n\n**The Rust translation is correct.** ~58.5 million differential call pairs through the FFI boundary, zero divergences.\n\nThe library is a single 12-line C function, `float ldexp_q2(float, int)`, and the translation faithfully reproduces every subtle behavior — including the C's undefined behavior, which I confirmed against the actual gcc disassembly (`sar %cl`, whose count x86 masks to 5 bits, matching the Rust's explicit `& 31`).\n\n## Two real problems found — both in the test apparatus, not the translation\n\n**1. The tests were silently passing against a stale `.so`.** `crate-type` was `[\"cdylib\"]` only, so integration tests had no Rust-level dependency on the lib target and **`cargo test` never rebuilt the `.so`**. Every \"passing\" differential assertion was comparing the C library against a build from 16 minutes earlier.\n\nI only caught this because I distrusted a suspicious result: 58M call pairs completing in ~1 second. I ran mutation testing to check the suite could actually fail — and **all 10 injected bugs escaped detection.** Fixed by adding `rlib` to `crate-type` (verified not to change the cdylib's exported ABI) plus an `assert_not_stale()` guard, which I confirmed fires by pointing it at a back-dated `.so`.\n\nHad I not checked, I would have reported a green suite that proved nothing.\n\n**2. Two of my own documented expectations were wrong, not the Rust.** My `ERRORS.md` predicted `exp_q2 == 119` → `0x30d744fd` (actual C: `0x309837f0`), and claimed a *scale* of 1 leaves subnormals untouched. It doesn't — the total multiplier is `frac[e&3] * 2^(30-k)`, so only `k == 0` is the identity. I corrected the docs to match the C rather than \"fixing\" the Rust.\n\n## Notable C behaviors replicated\n\n- Negative `exp_q2` is **periodic with period 128**, so `INT_MIN` is the identity while `-1..-4` annihilate `y` to a signed zero (and turn `±inf` into `0xFFC00000` via `inf * 0`).\n- `INT_MAX` drives the loop **17,895,698** times and still terminates.\n- sNaN quieting preserves the payload (`0x7FA00000 → 0x7FE00000`), distinct from the `inf*0` default indefinite — comparing by `to_bits()` rather than `==` was essential, since `NaN != NaN` would have let payload bugs pass silently.\n\n## Honest caveats\n\n- **There is no error surface.** The function is total: zero asserts, zero sentinels, one `return`, and no pointer/length/enum parameters. The null-pointer, oversized-length, and out-of-range-enum classes are structurally inapplicable; `ERRORS.md` documents this with grep evidence, and a test asserts it against the real header so the claim can't rot.\n- **Four mutations are provably semantics-preserving** and correctly not flagged. Two (`& 31` removed, widened to `& 63`) are no-ops in release but *panic in debug* — which is why the mask is load-bearing and why every configuration runs in both profiles.\n- The `& 31` masking matches gcc on **x86-64**; on an architecture where shifts don't mask to 5 bits, the C itself would behave differently.\n\nGate: clippy clean, symbol parity 1:1, all 28 `CONFIGS.md` and 18 `ERRORS.md` rows checked, 12/12 non-equivalent mutations caught, all 6 profile × feature configurations green (312 test executions). `c_src/` unmodified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":805,"cache_read_input_tokens":152556,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":805,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fceb0364-53bc-448b-bf88-91d6e77258b6","uuid":"a49c1e74-462f-4961-9d82-9f609c5ced47","timestamp":"2026-08-27T00:07:11.150Z"}
